    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Is it mostly about the central nervous system then? Like, is the plateau happening because the muscles are tired, or is it more that the brain/nerves just stop sending the signal as effectively? I'm trying to explain the \"why\" to my client so they don't feel like they're just wasting time.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right; once training intensity builds up, the central nervous system (CNS) fatigue is the primary barrier preventing further strength gains, often signaling itself as annoying queuing that trainees mistakenly assume is muscle soreness. The body signals to preserve itself by reducing motor unit recruitment when recovery isn't instantaneous enough to match the load, effectively stopping signal transfer for reasons of safety and efficiency. By taking a deload week, you are removing the metabolic byproducts that build up in the brain's motor cortex, which allows the function of neural drive to return to its highest potential without needing rest. This isn't \"wasting time\"; rather, it is essential for resetting the neural threshold so your muscles can again respond optimally to the same stimulus they were previously handling too hard for.",
