    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so when you talk about the body \"signaling to preserve itself,\" are there specific physiological signs I should look for in my client to know if the CNS is actually fried versus just regular muscle fatigue? feels like it's a pretty subtle distinction.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, distinguishing CNS fatigue from regular muscle soreness is crucial, as the former presents as a sharp drop in work capacity like struggling to maintain a set tempo or feeling an inability to produce force even with fresh legs, rather than loose joints or dull ache. Clients often report feeling \"stiff\" mentally and physically, finding their arms feel heavy during complex lifts in a way distinct from the usual tension from hard training. Conversely, active recovery usually resolves regular muscle fatigue within 24-48 hours, whereas a fried CNS can linger in peripheral form for several days or cause weakness upon reactivation. Identifying these subtle signs confirms that a true deload is necessary, validating the rest period as a direct intervention to re-enable optimal neural signaling rather than just passive waiting.",
